Root cause: orchestrate-agents-v2.ts uses `import 'dotenv/config'` at the top (line 28), which loads .env relative to process.cwd(). When the orchestrator is spawned by sprint-runner as a child process from the VPS (/home/invoica/apps/Invoica), process.cwd() is the VPS path, and the Mac .env at /Users/tarekmnif/kognai/.env is never loaded. Result: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY appears undefined → supervisor throws 'not set in .env' → supervisor returns score 0 every time.

Fix: Replace the `import 'dotenv/config'` at line 28 with an explicit dotenv load that resolves .env relative to the file's own __dirname, not process.cwd():

Replace:
  import 'dotenv/config';

With (add BEFORE existing imports, or replace the dotenv import line):
  import { config as dotenvConfig } from 'dotenv';
  import { resolve, dirname } from 'path';
  import { fileURLToPath } from 'url';
  const __filename = fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);
  const __filedirname = dirname(__filename);
  // Load .env from project root (one level up from scripts/)
  dotenvConfig({ path: resolve(__filedirname, '..', '.env') });

If the file uses CommonJS (not ESM), use __dirname directly:
  import { config as dotenvConfig } from 'dotenv';
  import { resolve } from 'path';
  dotenvConfig({ path: resolve(__dirname, '..', '.env') });

Check whether the file uses ESM (import/export) or CJS to pick the right variant. The file already uses `import` statements so it is likely ESM via ts-node. Keep the fix minimal — only change the dotenv load line. Do NOT change any other code.

The router_server.py (Sprint-063 TR-003-C) is now running on port 11435 but nothing calls it. Wire up assessTaskComplexity in scripts/orchestrate-agents-v2.ts to optionally call the HTTP router first.

Change: Make assessTaskComplexity async and add an HTTP router probe at the very top (before all existing heuristic logic):

  // HTTP router: if ROUTER_SERVER_URL is set, delegate routing to router_server.py
  const routerUrl = process.env.ROUTER_SERVER_URL || '';
  if (routerUrl) {
    try {
      const ac = new AbortController();
      const timer = setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 2000); // 2s timeout — never block
      const res = await fetch(`${routerUrl}/route`, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: JSON.stringify({ prompt: task.context || task.id, context_tokens: 0 }),
        signal: ac.signal,
      });
      clearTimeout(timer);
      if (res.ok) {
        const data = await res.json() as { tier: string; model_name: string; reasoning: string };
        const provider = (data.tier === 'cloud' || data.tier === 'apex') ? 'anthropic' : 'minimax';
        const model = (provider === 'anthropic') ? 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514' : 'MiniMax-M2.5';
        return { provider: provider as any, model, routingReason: `HTTP router: ${data.tier} — ${data.reasoning}` };
      }
    } catch { /* HTTP router unavailable — fall through to heuristics */ }
  }

This block goes AFTER the task_target switch block (Sprint-063) and BEFORE the heuristic signals. The existing assessTaskComplexity heuristics remain as the fallback.

